\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A vertex factor and an edge factor use different paths}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For a degree-zero scalar cochain #{f} and an
#{F}-valued cochain #{\beta}, the
[transported product](fdip-00SJ) has coefficients
#{(f\smile_U\beta)_J(x)=f(x)\beta_J(x)}, since the empty
path is the identity. For an #{F}-valued edge cochain
#{e_i\otimes\alpha_i} and an #{F'}-valued vertex cochain
#{\psi}, its coefficient is instead
#{\alpha_i(x)\otimes(T_i^{F'}\psi)(x)}.
For sign representations over #{\mathbb Z}, values
#{\alpha_i(x)=2}, #{\psi(x+\varepsilon_i)=3} and link
#{-1} give coefficient #{-6} under the usual multiplication
#{\mathbb Z\otimes\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z}.
The output has the tensor-product action, which is trivial
when both input actions are the sign action.}
